      "body": "### Project URL\n\nhttps://github.com/samyama-ai/samyama-graph\n\n### Category\n\nRust\n\n### Project Title\n\nA Rust graph database with built-in vector search\n\n### Project Description\n\nA graph database written in Rust that runs OpenCypher queries and vector search in a single engine. Setup is one docker pull, and it ships 11 case studies built from public data - cricket, IMDB, court judgments - so you can run real queries on day one.\n\n### Highlights\n\nMost projects in this space make you run two systems: a graph database for\nrelationships and a vector store for embeddings, kept in sync by your own\ncode. Samyama Graph runs OpenCypher traversal and HNSW vector search in one\nprocess, so a \"who is connected to whom\" query and a \"what is similar to\nthis\" query hit the same engine and the same data.\n\nIt is also easy to start, which matters more than the architecture for\nanyone trying it out. One docker pull from a public registry - no account,\nno signup, no sales call - and the case_studies directory has 11 datasets\nthat rebuild themselves from public sources: cricket ball-by-ball records,\nIMDB, Indian Supreme Court judgments, drug interactions, protein pathways.\nEach one ships its loader and its queries, so you can go from empty\ndatabase to a real answer in an evening.\n\nApache-2.0, written in Rust, 14 graph algorithms. The benchmark page\npublishes its own unflattering results, including two runs of the same\nbinary that differed by 24%.\n\n### Example Code\n\n```markdown\n// Which bowler has dismissed which batsman most often?\n// 36,619 nodes and 1,392,017 edges of ball-by-ball cricket data.\nMATCH (bowler:Player)-[d:DISMISSED]->(batsman:Player)\nRETURN bowler.name, batsman.name, count(d) AS times\nORDER BY times DESC\nLIMIT 5\n```\n\n### Screenshots or Demo Videos\n\nA one-minute demo of Claude querying six of these knowledge graphs over MCP,\npicking the server and writing the Cypher itself:\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YQDtzhbR8ns\n\nThe eleven case studies, each with its loader and queries:\nhttps://github.com/samyama-ai/samyama-graph/tree/main/case_studies",
